{"auditVerdictAtHead":"pass","canonicalDigest":"sha256:b709ed882f70190de3357b9075d80207749deda7995ee160138a4b716777078c","canonicalizationVersion":"1","changeRequest":{"author":"buger","body":"**TL;DR:** If a menu route is summoned while the shell is starting (for example Super+Escape or the power key right after login, or just after `omarchy-restart-shell`, which `omarchy update` runs), the menu opens root and stays there. With this change it opens the route you asked for once both menu files have loaded, so your own overrides apply. An action alias summoned in that window (Super+Ctrl+R, Super+Ctrl+Space, Super+Shift+Ctrl+Space) now runs its action; before, you got root. If you start typing or open another menu before the files load, the route is dropped. Summons after startup behave as before. The change is 21 lines in `Menu.qml`, 7 of them comments, plus one test file. Fixes #14053.\n\n66-second narrated run in the real shell: quattro 821ae58 opens the main menu for a cold-start `omarchy menu summon system`, the fix (recorded at 43d787d; the second commit does not change this case) opens System, and a summon after startup opens System on both.\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5964f787-ac08-464d-97e3-2aa17dab9299\n\n### What happens on quattro (821ae58)\n\nThe shell queues a summon that arrives before the menu plugin has loaded, and hands it to `open()` as soon as the plugin loads (`shell/shell.qml:1171-1179`, `:1221-1226`). Neither JSONC `FileView` has read its file yet. The user extension usually answers first, a few ms before the default file.\n\n- `openRoute()` can't resolve the route, so `openExistingMenu()` falls back to root (`Menu.qml:846`).\n- When the files load, `rebuildItemsFromSources()` redraws root. Nothing opens the route that was asked for.\n- `openRoute()` writes the route to `pendingInitialMenu` (`:911`), but nothing reads it any more.\n\n### The change\n\n`pendingInitialMenu` was meant for this. The bash menu's `menuJsonFileView.onLoaded` used to open it once the menu data arrived. That reader went away with the bash menu in edfd68d4 (\"Strip the bash-bin compatibility layer from Menu.qml\"), and since 4cd6593b (\"Use plugin IPC routes\") the property has only been written. This PR reads it again:\n\n- `openExistingMenu()` keeps the route pending only when it fell back to root while the menu files are still loading.\n- `menuFilesAnswered()` is true once the default file has loaded and the user file has loaded or failed (it is optional, so a missing file counts as an answer). `FileView.loaded` is false until the first read lands; the user file's `onLoadFailed` sets `userMenuFailed`.\n- At the end of `rebuildItemsFromSources()`, which both `FileView`s call, a pending route gets one try once both files have answered, then it is cleared whatever happens. It opens through `openRoute()`, so aliases, links and action aliases work as they do after startup, and with the user's overrides applied.\n- `setFilter()` and `setActiveMenu()` drop a pending route, so typing or opening another menu (including going back to root) wins over a late load.\n\nNothing changes after startup. A route summoned after both files have answered never waits, and live edits of either JSONC file reload exactly as before.\n\n### How to check\n\n```bash\nbash test/shell.d/menu-startup-route-test.sh   # 27 ok; on quattro 821ae58 it stops at its first check (Menu.qml has no menuFilesAnswered)\nbash test/shell.d/menu-test.sh                 # 127 ok, unchanged\n```\n\nThe new test runs the real `open`, `openRoute`, `openExistingMenu`, `openDmenu`, `cancel`, `rebuildItemsFromSources`, `menuFilesAnswered`, `setFilter`, `setActiveMenu` and `goBack` bodies, and the `FileView` handlers, from `Menu.qml` in a node `vm` context. It feeds them the shipped `omarchy-menu.jsonc` files. The pattern is the same as in `color-reference-test.sh`. It covers:\n\n- **Load orders:** the default file first, the missing user file first, the stock user extension first.\n- **Routes:** a menu that only the user file defines, an alias, an action alias (runs once, then the menu closes), and an action the user file overrides (runs the user's command, and nothing runs before the user file has answered).\n- **Things that must not replay:** no route, a missing id (also when a later edit adds it), a summon after startup for a menu a later edit adds, a summon closed before the load, a select prompt that replaced the summon, a user who opened another menu, went into one and back to root, or typed, a second reload, and a summon after startup.\n\nOn the first commit, 43d787d, the test stops at the first of the 7 checks for the two cases above (overrides; navigating back / later edits). Each condition in the change is pinned: removing any one of them fails a check.\n\nIn the real shell (Quickshell under a private headless sway, cold start, summon retried until the shell accepts it, 3 runs each):\n\n| Summon during startup | 821ae58 | this PR |\n|---|---|---|\n| `system` (no user extension, the stock one, or one with items) | root | System |\n| `power-menu` (alias) | root | System |\n| a menu that only the user extension defines | root | that menu |\n| an action alias from the user extension | root, action not run | action runs once, menu closes |\n| `about` overridden in the user file, default file loading first (user file read slowed to 1.5 s) | root, nothing runs | the user's command runs (the first commit ran the default one) |\n| a menu summoned during startup; the user goes into Learn and back to root before the user file loads (read slowed to 4 s) | root | root (the first commit jumped to the summoned menu) |\n| a menu nobody defines, summoned after startup, then added by editing the user file | root | root (the first commit opened it) |\n| a missing id / no route | root | root |\n| the same summons after startup | the requested menu | the requested menu |\n\nThe same holds when the user file appears after the default file, and when the default file appears after the user file. Across the 54 cold runs, the summon reached the menu 1–11 ms before the user file and 3–16 ms before the default file, and the user file always answered first, 2–7 ms before the default file.\n\nOn a cold start the menu sits on root for a moment before the route opens: 10–18 ms in the shell logs, which is less than a frame. No frame of the recording shows it. When the default file loads first, root stays up until the user file answers.\n\nLeft as is: arrow keys or the pointer on root before the files load don't cancel the route (only typing and opening a menu do); a summon that lands between the two loads resolves at once against what has loaded, as it did before; with no default menu file at all, a fallen-back route never opens.\n\n<details><summary>Other open PRs that touch the same code</summary>\n\n- **#13043 / #13246** change the `openRoute()` / `openExistingMenu()` signatures to carry `initialIndex` / `initialId`. This PR touches neither those lines nor `open()`, and it merges cleanly with both. One semantic overlap: the startup replay calls `openRoute(pending)` with the route only. With #13043 in, a named starting row would be lost for a summon made during startup. Whichever lands second can carry the row through `pendingInitialMenu`. Happy to do that here if #13043 lands first.\n- **#7413** changes the `if (root.opened)` line in `rebuildItemsFromSources()`. The replay sits after that block, so the two merge cleanly.\n- **#14054** (uninstall confirmation) merges cleanly, and both test files pass on the combined tree.\n- **#10007 and #7345** rewrite the user file's `onLoadFailed` line, which this PR now edits to set `userMenuFailed`, so each gets a one-line conflict. A separate handler wouldn't avoid it: it would run after the rebuild and miss the replay.\n- More broadly, of the 91 open PRs that touch `Menu.qml`, 70 merge cleanly with quattro; 68 of them still do with this PR (the other two are #10007 and #7345), and the remaining 21 already conflict with quattro.\n\n</details>\n\n**Independent verification:** [the evidence for this PR](https://portal.reqproof.com/projects/omarchy/changes/pr/14055) comes from a full proof audit of `4840566` merged onto `quattro` at `393a43d` (evaluated merge `5f56be0`): test report 59 passed · 0 failed · 0 skipped, plus 13 tests observed by the behaviour comparison run: 13 passed.\n\n","bodyDigest":"sha256:f1397ff4fca19faf0d7c352c99ca5faa7da59a89331ce7be1ccd06f23c162580","bodyExcerpt":"**TL;DR:** If a menu route is summoned while the shell is starting (for example Super+Escape or the power key right after login, or just after `omarchy-restart-shell`, which `omarchy update` runs), the menu opens root and stays there. With this change it opens the route you asked for once both menu files have loaded, so your own overrides apply. An action alias summoned in that window (Super+Ctrl+R, Super+Ctrl+Space, Super+Shift+Ctrl+Space) now runs its action; before, you got root. If you start typing or open another menu before the files load, the route is dropped. Summons after startup beha…","linkedIssues":["#14053"],"source":"explicit","title":"Open a menu route summoned during startup once the menu files load"},"createdAt":"2026-10-04T17:21:31Z","decisionSummary":{"claimEvidence":[{"claim":"requirement_preserved","claimId":"requirement:SW-REQ-260922-3JG5","failed":0,"notRun":0,"obligations":[{"class":"boundary","failed":0,"observedVia":"SYS-REQ-260922-P708","passed":1,"skipped":0,"source":"inherited:SYS-REQ-260922-P708","status":"observed_pass","tests":1}],"passed":2,"requirementId":"SW-REQ-260922-3JG5","status":"observed_pass","unknown":0},{"claim":"requirement_preserved","claimId":"requirement:SW-REQ-260922-3T3F","failed":0,"notRun":0,"obligations":[{"class":"boundary","failed":0,"passed":2,"skipped":0,"source":"own","status":"observed_pass","tests":2},{"class":"error_handling","failed":0,"passed":2,"skipped":0,"source":"own","status":"observe